from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "7. The Fire and the Worm: Mark 9:43–48 and Isaiah 66:24"),
("p", "Claim: the Lord’s words about a worm that dieth not and a fire that is not quenched are quoted from Isaiah, and Isaiah’s verse is a picture of corpses. The volume says so first. The Lord’s use of the picture is not Isaiah’s use, because He sets it before living persons as a place into which they may be cast, and the volume reads His words for what they state of the persons and not for what the fire consumes."),
("sub", "7.1 The setting of Mark 9"),
("p", "The words are spoken at Capernaum. The Twelve had disputed by the way about which of them should be greatest, and the Lord “sat down, and called the twelve”, and set a child in the midst of them (Mark 9:33–36). He then warns of offending “one of these little ones that believe in me”, and says that for such an offender “it is better for him that a millstone were hanged about his neck, and he were cast into the sea” (Mark 9:42). The warning that follows is addressed to the disciples and is spoken in the Kingdom ministry, in the same chapter in which the Lord says that some standing there would not taste of death “till they have seen the kingdom of God come with power” (Mark 9:1). The volume reads the warning in that setting. It is the Lord’s word to Israel’s disciples about the fate that awaits those who cause the little ones to stumble, and it is not applied to the Body of Christ."),
("p", "The warning is built on three examples: the hand, the foot and the eye. Of the hand the Lord says, “if thy hand offend thee, cut it off: it is better for thee to enter into life maimed, than having two hands to go into hell, into the fire that never shall be quenched: Where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not quenched” (Mark 9:43, 44). The foot and the eye follow in the same form, and each ends with the same clause. The King James Version prints the clause three times, at verses 44, 46 and 48. Some editions do not print it at verses 44 and 46, and the volume does not rest on them. Verse 48 stands in every edition, and it is enough: “Where their worm dieth not, and the fire is not quenched” (Mark 9:48)."),
("sub", "7.2 What the verses state"),
("p", "Four things are stated. The first is that the choice is between two places: to “enter into life” (verse 43) or “into the kingdom of God” (verse 47) maimed, or to “be cast into hell” whole. The second is that the place of the second choice is called hell, the word the Gospels use for the valley of Hinnom, and that in it there is a fire “that never shall be quenched”. The third is that the worm is spoken of as belonging to the persons: “their worm”. The fourth is that the verse says of it that it “dieth not”, and says of the fire that it “is not quenched”. The sentence is in the present tense, and it does not say that the fire will go out or that the worm will end."),
("p", "Three things are not stated. The verses do not say that those cast into hell cease to be. They do not say that they are conscious. They do not describe what the worm does to them. The words are a picture, and the Lord gives no interpretation of the picture beyond the words. It is for this reason that the origin of the picture matters, and the volume turns to it."),
("sub", "7.3 Isaiah 66:24"),
("p", "The clause is the last verse of Isaiah’s prophecy. The prophet has been speaking of the new heavens and the new earth, which “shall remain before me, saith the LORD, so shall your seed and your name remain” (Isaiah 66:22). The nations are gathered to see the Lord’s glory, and His own people are brought from all nations to “my holy mountain Jerusalem” (Isaiah 66:20). “And it shall come to pass, that from one new moon to another, and from one sabbath to another, shall all flesh come to worship before me, saith the LORD” (Isaiah 66:23). The last verse follows: “And they shall go forth, and look upon the carcases of the men that have transgressed against me: for their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched; and they shall be an abhorring unto all flesh” (Isaiah 66:24)."),
("p", "The verse is a picture of corpses, and the volume says so without qualification. The word is “carcases”. The persons who go forth are the worshippers of the Lord, they look at what lies there, and what they see is dead men with a worm and a fire at their work. An annihilationist reader takes this as the clearest evidence in the Old Testament that the end of the wicked is their destruction, and the argument has force. A worm that feeds on a dead body and a fire that burns it are agents of consumption, and nothing in the verse says that the body is alive."),
("p", "The second point on the other side is about the word “quenched”. In the Old Testament a fire that is not quenched is a fire that no one can put out, and it burns until what it burns is finished. Of Jerusalem the Lord says, “then will I kindle a fire in the gates thereof, and it shall devour the palaces of Jerusalem, and it shall not be quenched” (Jeremiah 17:27). Of the forest of the south, “the flaming flame shall not be quenched, and all faces from the south to the north shall be burned therein” (Ezekiel 20:47). The fire in Jerusalem’s gates is not burning today, and the unquenched fire of Ezekiel’s forest did its work and ended. John’s words at the Jordan of the one who comes after him carry the same idea: he “will burn up the chaff with unquenchable fire” (Matthew 3:12), and chaff is burned up. The volume grants all of this. An unquenchable fire is a fire that cannot be put out, and the phrase does not say that what is thrown into it never burns away."),
("tbl", "Where a fire is said not to be quenched.", [
    ("Jeremiah 17:27", T("Jer 17:27"), "A fire in the gates of Jerusalem that devours the palaces and is not quenched; the palaces are consumed."),
    ("Ezekiel 20:47", T("Ezek 20:47"), "A flaming flame in the forest that is not quenched, and the trees are burned."),
    ("Matthew 3:12", T("Matt 3:12"), "The chaff is burned up with unquenchable fire; the fire is not said to continue after the chaff."),
    ("Isaiah 66:24", T("Isa 66:24"), "The worm shall not die and the fire shall not be quenched, and the persons are described as carcases."),
    ("Mark 9:48", T("Mark 9:48"), "The same clause, set in the Lord’s warning to His disciples about being cast into hell."),
  ], [0.2, 0.45, 0.35]),
("sub", "7.4 What the Lord does with the verse"),
("p", "Nothing so far makes Mark 9:48 say more than Isaiah 66:24. The question is whether the Lord uses the clause as the prophet does, and the volume holds that He does not. The prophet’s scene is of persons who have died and are seen by others. The Lord’s scene is of persons who are alive and who are warned against the casting. “It is better for thee to enter into life maimed, than having two hands to go into hell” is a sentence about a living man who has two hands, and the choice is between going into life without a hand and going into hell with both. The man goes into the fire as himself, and the clause that follows says what is at the place: their worm, and a fire. The Lord does not say that they are seen as carcases. He says they are “cast into hell”, and the picture that He takes from Isaiah is a picture of what is there."),
("p", "The Lord had already used the same warning in the Sermon on the Mount: “it is profitable for thee that one of thy members should perish, and not that thy whole body should be cast into hell” (Matthew 5:29, 30). The member perishes, and the whole body is cast into hell. These are not the same thing in the sentence, and the second is described as what the first prevents. In the same breath He says, “whosoever shall say, Thou fool, shall be in danger of hell fire” (Matthew 5:22). The place is named in the Gospels as a place into which persons are cast, and the body is the object of the casting."),
("p", "This is Inferred, and the volume marks it. The Lord does not say whether the persons in the place are conscious or whether their worm feeds on what is alive. A reader who holds that the Lord’s picture is the same as Isaiah’s, a picture of the finished destruction of those who are gone, can say so, and the words do not forbid it. The volume holds that the Lord’s sentence is more naturally read as placing the persons in the fire and the worm, and that the “their” of the worm and the “cast” of the persons are the Lord’s own additions to what the prophet had said. It does not rest the case on the verse."),
("sub", "7.5 The valley and the picture"),
("p", "The name of the place carries a history. The valley of the son of Hinnom was the place where the people of Judah “built the high places of Tophet… to burn their sons and their daughters in the fire”, and the Lord said that it should be called “the valley of slaughter”, where “the carcases of this people shall be meat for the fowls of the heaven, and for the beasts of the earth” (Jeremiah 7:31, 33). The prophet gives the same name again in the nineteenth chapter, in the setting of judgment on the city. The Lord’s choice of the name Gehenna, when He speaks of the place of the fire, brings the picture of that valley with it: a place of unburied dead and of fire. The volume grants that this gives the picture a corpse-like colour and that it favours the other side’s reading of the image."),
("p", "The Lord does not use the name only for the picture. He says to the scribes and Pharisees, “Ye serpents, ye generation of vipers, how can ye escape the damnation of hell?” (Matthew 23:33). The damnation of hell is a judgment of persons who are living and who are addressed. In the Sermon on the Mount He says that the offender is “in danger of hell fire” (Matthew 5:22), and in the commission to the Twelve He says that God is able “to destroy both soul and body in hell” (Matthew 10:28). In each place the persons are the ones who are judged, and the place is the place of judgment. The valley supplies the picture and the sentence supplies the persons."),
("sub", "7.6 The oracle of Edom and what it shows"),
("p", "One more Old Testament passage belongs in the chapter, because it is the other side’s best parallel. In Isaiah 34 the Lord’s indignation is “upon all nations”, and His sword “shall come down upon Idumea, and upon the people of my curse, to judgment” (Isaiah 34:2, 5). The land is described: “the streams thereof shall be turned into pitch, and the dust thereof into brimstone, and the land thereof shall become burning pitch. It shall not be quenched night nor day; the smoke thereof shall go up for ever: from generation to generation it shall lie waste” (Isaiah 34:9, 10). The fire of the oracle is a fire that does not go out and a smoke that goes up for ever, and the land of Edom is not burning today. The idiom is a way of saying that the ruin is permanent."),
("p", "The volume grants the point in full and draws the line where it falls. Isaiah’s smoke that goes up for ever is the smoke of a land and a nation, and what it says is that the desolation is not undone. The oracle does not describe persons tormented, and the volume does not use it to say that they are. It shows that Scripture speaks of a fire that does not go out and a smoke that does not cease in connection with a judgment that finished its object, and that the words “for ever” in such a place describe the permanence of the ruin. Where Revelation takes up the same imagery of persons, as it does in chapter 14, the words are the words of persons, with “no rest day nor night”. The Edom oracle is cited in the Prophets Series, in Volume 3, The Nations Judged, with its own question, and this volume takes only the idiom from it."),
("sub", "7.7 Weeping and gnashing of teeth"),
("p", "The Lord’s account of the end uses another phrase that bears on what is at the place. Of the wheat and the tares He says, “The Son of man shall send forth his angels, and they shall gather out of his kingdom all things that offend, and them which do iniquity; And shall cast them into a furnace of fire: there shall be wailing and gnashing of teeth” (Matthew 13:41, 42). The same sentence is spoken of the dragnet in the next parable (Matthew 13:50). It is said of the children of the kingdom who are cast out (Matthew 8:12), of the man without a wedding garment (Matthew 22:13), of the unprofitable servant (Matthew 25:30) and of the servant who is cut asunder and given his portion with the hypocrites (Matthew 24:51). In Luke it is said of those who see Abraham, Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom of God “and you yourselves thrust out” (Luke 13:28)."),
("p", "The phrase is an expression of a person’s distress. It is not what happens to the fuel of a fire. The tares are burned in the fire (Matthew 13:40), and the volume does not build on the parable’s picture of burning. It notes that the Lord places the wailing at the furnace: “there shall be wailing”. The word “there” is the word that tells the reader that what is described is a condition at the place, and a condition of persons who are able to wail. This is shown by the context. The parables are read in their own place in the Parable Studies, and this volume uses them only for the phrase."),
("ctbl", "What the fire passages say of the persons and what they leave out.", [
    ("Passage", "What is said of the persons", "What is not said"),
    ("Isaiah 66:24", "They are carcases, seen by the worshippers; the worm and the fire are with them", "That they are alive; that they are conscious"),
    ("Mark 9:43–48", "A man with two hands is cast into hell, into the fire that is not quenched; their worm dieth not", "What the worm and the fire do; whether the person is conscious"),
    ("Matthew 5:29, 30", "The whole body is cast into hell, set against the loss of a member", "How long the body is there"),
    ("Matthew 13:41, 42", "They are cast into a furnace of fire; there is wailing and gnashing of teeth", "Whether the wailing is for a moment or for ever"),
    ("Isaiah 34:9, 10", "A land is turned to burning pitch; the smoke goes up for ever", "Any person tormented; the oracle is of a land"),
  ], [0.2, 0.42, 0.38]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Mark 9:43–48 is the Lord’s warning to His disciples in the Kingdom ministry, and it quotes the last verse of Isaiah.",
    "Isaiah 66:24 is a picture of corpses; the volume grants that it supports a reading in which the fire and the worm consume the dead.",
    "A fire that is not quenched is a fire that cannot be put out; Jeremiah 17:27, Ezekiel 20:47 and Matthew 3:12 show that the fire may finish its work, and the volume grants it.",
    "The Lord does not say “carcases”; He says that a living man with two hands is cast into hell, and that their worm dieth not.",
    "The valley of Hinnom gives the picture a corpse-like colour, and the Lord’s use of the name for persons judged is a use of the name for the place of judgment.",
    "The Edom oracle shows that a smoke that goes up for ever can be the permanence of a ruin; it describes a land and tells nothing of persons tormented.",
    "Weeping and gnashing of teeth is set at the place of casting out, and it is the distress of persons.",
  ]),
]
